const contentHtml = `<p class="lead">Most contractor websites are expensive digital brochures. They look fine, list services, and have a contact page — but they convert just 1% to 3% of visitors. The best contractor websites convert at 11% or higher. The difference is not traffic. It is design.</p>
<p>This guide covers the website design decisions that turn home service visitors into qualified leads. Whether you run a roofing, HVAC, plumbing, electrical, or restoration company, these principles apply.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What a Contractor Website Must Accomplish</h2>
<p>A contractor website has one primary job: generate qualified leads. Everything else — aesthetics, animations, clever copy — is secondary. To do that, it must accomplish five things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Generate leads:</strong> Clear phone numbers, short forms, and multiple conversion points.</li>
<li><strong>Build trust immediately:</strong> Licenses, insurance, reviews, and real project photos.</li>
<li><strong>Show service areas clearly:</strong> City names, maps, and ZIP codes.</li>
<li><strong>Enable emergency contact:</strong> Prominent click-to-call, especially on mobile.</li>
<li><strong>Support SEO:</strong> Fast load times, proper headings, schema markup, and mobile-first design.</li>
</ul>
<p>When any of these are missing, visitors bounce to a competitor who makes the next step easier.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Homepage Design That Converts</h2>
<p>Visitors decide whether to stay or leave within 50 milliseconds. Your homepage must answer three questions in the first three seconds:</p>
<ol>
<li>What do you do?</li>
<li>Do you serve my area?</li>
<li>How do I contact you?</li>
</ol>
<p>Your headline should be specific: "Emergency Plumbing in Phoenix — 24/7 Response, Licensed & Insured" beats "Welcome to Our Website." Your above-the-fold section needs a click-to-call phone number, a short form, and a clear primary service.</p>
<p>Include trust signals near the headline: years in business, review rating, local certifications, and a satisfaction guarantee. These small details increase conversions by up to 28%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Service Pages and Location Pages</h2>
<p>Every major service deserves its own page. A single "Services" page with a bullet list does not rank and does not convert. Build dedicated pages for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Roof replacement, roof repair, storm damage</li>
<li>HVAC repair, installation, maintenance</li>
<li>Emergency plumbing, drain cleaning, water heater replacement</li>
<li>Commercial services</li>
</ul>
<p>Each service page should explain the problem you solve, your process, what makes you different, pricing guidance where possible, and a clear CTA.</p>
<p>If you serve multiple cities, create location pages for each. A page titled "Roofing Contractor in [City], [State]" with unique local content, project photos from that area, and city-specific reviews will outrank generic service pages.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mobile-First Design Is Non-Negotiable</h2>
<p>Google uses mobile-first indexing exclusively. Over 60% of local service searches happen on mobile. Yet many contractor websites still bury the phone number in a menu or load a desktop hero image that takes five seconds on a phone.</p>
<p>Your mobile site must:</p>
<ul>
<li>Display the phone number and "Call Now" button above the fold</li>
<li>Load the largest contentful paint in under 2.5 seconds</li>
<li>Use thumb-friendly buttons and forms</li>
<li>Show emergency CTAs prominently for 24/7 trades</li>
</ul>
<p>A one-second delay in mobile load time can reduce conversions by 7%. On a $1M revenue business, that is roughly $140,000 in lost revenue.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Trust Signals That Increase Conversions</h2>
<p>Homeowners invite contractors into their homes. They need proof you are trustworthy before they call. Place these signals throughout your site:</p>
<ul>
<li>Real customer reviews with names and locations</li>
<li>Before-and-after project galleries</li>
<li>License and insurance badges</li>
<li>Manufacturer certifications (GAF, Owens Corning, etc.)</li>
<li>BBB rating and industry associations</li>
<li>Video testimonials</li>
</ul>
<p>Social proof should not be hidden on a testimonials page. It belongs on your homepage, service pages, and location pages — wherever a visitor is making a decision.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Technical Requirements</h2>
<p>A beautiful design fails if Google cannot crawl it or visitors abandon it while loading. Your site needs:</p>
<ul>
<li>HTTPS security</li>
<li>Fast hosting with a CDN</li>
<li>Compressed images and lazy loading</li>
<li>Clean heading hierarchy (one H1 per page)</li>
<li>Descriptive URLs and meta descriptions</li>
<li>LocalBusiness schema and Service schema</li>
<li>An XML sitemap submitted to Google Search Console</li>
</ul>
<p>These elements do not just improve rankings. They improve user experience, which feeds back into rankings and conversions.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Choosing the Right Platform</h2>
<p>Most contractors choose between WordPress, Webflow, Wix, and Squarespace. For serious lead generation, we recommend WordPress or Webflow. WordPress offers the most flexibility and plugin ecosystem. Webflow offers cleaner code, faster performance, and easier design control.</p>
<p>Wix and Squarespace work for very small operations but often fall short on speed, schema flexibility, and local SEO depth as you scale.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Your 30-Day Action Plan</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Week 1:</strong> Audit your homepage. Add a specific headline, click-to-call button, and lead form above the fold.</li>
<li><strong>Week 2:</strong> Create or improve service pages. Add process, proof, and CTAs.</li>
<li><strong>Week 3:</strong> Build location pages for your top five service cities.</li>
<li><strong>Week 4:</strong> Improve mobile speed and add schema markup.</li>
</ul>
<p>Small improvements in each area compound quickly. A contractor who moves from 2% to 5% conversion often doubles revenue without increasing ad spend.</p>
